---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { profile } from "../../data/profile";

// Unlisted on purpose: this page is linked from App Store Connect, not from the site.
// Layout emits noindex, and public/robots.txt disallows /hiking-passport/.
const updated = "September 14, 2026";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy, Hiking Passport"
  description="How Hiking Passport handles your Apple Health data, your training plans, and your purchases."
  noindex={true}
>
  <section class="post legal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="post-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Hiking Passport</p>
        <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Your health data stays between you and Apple. This page spells out
          exactly what Hiking Passport reads, what leaves your iPhone, and how
          to turn any of it off.
        </p>
        <p class="org">Last updated: {updated}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="post-body">
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>
          Hiking Passport is an iOS app that builds a personal training plan for
          the hike you are working toward, and shows your progress as a picture
          instead of a chart. To do that it reads some of your Apple Health
          data, with your permission, and uses Apple Intelligence to turn it
          into a plan.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no account to create and no login. I do not run a server that
          stores your health data, and I never sell anyone's personal
          information.
        </p>

        <h2>What the app reads from Apple Health</h2>
        <p>
          The first time you open Hiking Passport it asks permission to read the
          following from Apple Health. You choose what to allow, and you can
          change your mind at any time.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Workouts, steps, distance, flights climbed, and active energy, so
            the plan knows what you already do in a week
          </li>
          <li>
            Heart rate, resting heart rate, and cardio fitness, so the plan
            matches your current conditioning
          </li>
          <li>Sleep, so the plan can ease off on low energy days</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The app reads this data. It does not change anything in Apple Health,
          and it does not read anything outside the list above. If you decline a
          category, the app still works, the plan is just less tailored.
        </p>

        <h2>How your plan is generated</h2>
        <p>
          Plans are generated with Apple Intelligence. Simple requests run
          entirely on your iPhone. Longer ones are sent to Apple's Private Cloud
          Compute, which is Apple's own infrastructure built for exactly this.
        </p>
        <p>
          When a request goes to Private Cloud Compute, Apple uses it only to
          return your plan. It is not stored afterwards, it is not accessible to
          Apple, and it is not used to train any model. You can read Apple's
          description of how that works on{" "}
          <a href="https://security.apple.com/documentation/private-cloud-compute/"
            >Apple's Private Cloud Compute page</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          What gets sent is the context the plan needs, such as a summary of
          your recent activity and the goal and constraints you entered. Your
          name, your email, and your raw Apple Health history are not sent.
        </p>

        <h2>What you tell the app directly</h2>
        <p>
          You can tell Hiking Passport about trail access, a disability, an
          injury, a busy schedule, or anything else that shapes how you train.
          That is sensitive information and it is treated that way. It is stored
          on your iPhone, it is included in a plan request only when the plan
          depends on it, and it is never sent anywhere else.
        </p>

        <h2>Purchases</h2>
        <p>
          Subscriptions are handled by Apple and by{" "}
          <a href="https://www.revenuecat.com/privacy/">RevenueCat</a>, which
          keeps track of whether your subscription is active. RevenueCat
          receives an anonymous identifier for your install, your purchase and
          receipt status, and basic device and platform information. It does not
          receive your name, your email, your Apple Health data, or your plans.
        </p>
        <p>
          Apple handles the payment itself. I never see your card details.
        </p>

        <h2>Analytics and crash reports</h2>
        <p>
          The app collects anonymous, aggregated usage metrics and crash reports
          so I can find bugs and see which features people actually use. This
          data cannot be used to identify you, and it never includes your Apple
          Health data, your plans, or anything you typed about your body, your
          health, or your schedule.
        </p>

        <h2>What never leaves your iPhone</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Your raw Apple Health history</li>
          <li>The photos you use for your progress image</li>
          <li>Anything you wrote about an injury, a disability, or your energy</li>
          <li>Your saved plans and your completed hikes</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Your choices</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Turn off Health access.</strong> Open the Health app, tap your
            picture, then Privacy, then Apps, then Hiking Passport. Turn off any
            category, or all of them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Turn off Apple Intelligence.</strong> In iOS Settings, under
            Apple Intelligence. The app keeps working, and plans fall back to
            what you set yourself.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Delete everything.</strong> Deleting the app removes the data
            it stored on your iPhone. Your Apple Health data belongs to Apple
            Health and stays there.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Ask me to delete a purchase record.</strong> Email me and I
            will have RevenueCat delete the record tied to your install.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>
          Hiking Passport is not directed to children under 13, and I do not
          knowingly collect their information. If you think a child gave me
          theirs, email me and I will delete it.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
          If the app starts handling your information differently, I will update
          this page and change the date at the top. The version you are reading
          was last updated on {updated}.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions, deletion requests, or anything else about your privacy:{" "}
          <a href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>{profile.email}</a>. Hiking
          Passport is made by Lilly Seay in Portland, Oregon.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
